@@@ section
{"id": "2d", "title": "Arrays of Pointers & 2D Memory", "summary": "Matrices, jagged arrays and string arrays: memory with several levels of ownership, and freeing it in the right order."}

@@@ problem allocate-a-matrix
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Allocate and Free a Matrix",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "topics": ["malloc", "2D arrays", "free"],
  "params": [{"name": "rows", "type": "int"}, {"name": "cols", "type": "int"}, {"name": "value", "type": "int"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["2", "3", "7"], "output": "[[7,7,7],[7,7,7]]", "explanation": "createMatrix(2, 3, 7) builds the matrix; then the harness calls your freeMatrix and checks that nothing leaked."},
    {"input": ["1", "1", "0"], "output": "[[0]]"}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "One allocation for the array of row pointers, plus one per row.",
    "freeMatrix must free every row first, then the array of row pointers.",
    "If you free the row array first, reading matrix[r] afterwards is a use-after-free."
  ],
  "constraints": ["1 <= rows, cols <= 100"],
  "memory": "`createMatrix` allocates; `freeMatrix` must release **everything** `createMatrix` allocated. Any block left over is reported as a leak."
}
@@ description
Write a pair of functions: one that builds a `rows x cols` matrix of `int`s filled with `value`, and one that destroys it.

```c
int **createMatrix(int rows, int cols, int value);
void freeMatrix(int **matrix, int rows);
```

The harness calls `createMatrix`, prints the matrix through `matrix[r][c]`, then calls **your** `freeMatrix` and checks that every byte was returned.

The usual layout is an **array of row pointers**: `matrix` points to `rows` pointers, and `matrix[r]` points to that row's `cols` integers. So `matrix[r][c]` first follows `matrix[r]`, then indexes into the row.

Pairs like `create`/`free`, `open`/`close` or `init`/`destroy` are how C libraries hand out resources: whoever calls `create` must eventually call the matching `free`, and only that function knows how the memory was laid out.
@@ prelude
int **createMatrix(int rows, int cols, int value);
void freeMatrix(int **matrix, int rows);
@@ starter
#include <stdlib.h>

int **createMatrix(int rows, int cols, int value) {
    // TODO: allocate the row pointers, then each row.
    return NULL;
}

void freeMatrix(int **matrix, int rows) {
    // TODO: free what createMatrix allocated.
}
@@ solution
#include <stdlib.h>

int **createMatrix(int rows, int cols, int value) {
    int **m = malloc(rows * sizeof *m);
    if (m == NULL) return NULL;
    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
        m[r] = malloc(cols * sizeof *m[r]);
        if (m[r] == NULL) {
            while (r-- > 0) free(m[r]);     /* undo what was allocated */
            free(m);
            return NULL;
        }
        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
            m[r][c] = value;
    }
    return m;
}

void freeMatrix(int **matrix, int rows) {
    if (matrix == NULL) return;
    for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
        free(matrix[r]);
    free(matrix);
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        int rows = lc_read_int(), cols = lc_read_int(), value = lc_read_int();
        int **m = createMatrix(rows, cols, value);
        int r;
        if (!m) {
            lc_fail("createMatrix returned NULL.");
            lc_out_null();
        } else {
            lc_out_begin();
            for (r = 0; r < rows; r++) lc_out_int_array(m[r], cols);
            lc_out_end();
            freeMatrix(m, rows);
        }
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
